 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Training time is comparable to or slightly faster than standard 3DGS. On Mip-NeRF360, AtomGS averages 0.28 hours vs 3DGS's 0.40 hours [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Model size is reduced. On Mip-NeRF360, AtomGS results in 749MB vs 3DGS's 869MB [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Scaling:** The method stabilizes Gaussian count, leading to a declining and stabilizing trend, which improves memory efficiency over time compared to the fluctuating count in standard 3DGS [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Enhances geometric precision, reduces noisy meshes and blurry artifacts, stabilizes densification, and compactly models smooth surfaces while retaining fine details [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires careful tuning of $\varepsilon$ for different datasets (e.g., DTU vs Mip-NeRF360). The atomization process adds complexity to the training loop.
+*   **Time Complexity:** Similar to standard 3DGS densification, but with additional checks for atomization and geometric progression updates. The warm-up phase may increase early iteration cost due to aggressive cloning/splitting.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Initially higher due to aggressive densification, but stabilizes and often results in fewer total Gaussians than standard 3DGS by merging smooth surfaces. On Mip-NeRF360, AtomGS achieved 749MB vs 3DGS's 869MB [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Measured Performance:** On Mip-NeRF360, average training time 0.28 hours vs 3DGS's 0.40 hours [S1, unofficial]. Superior quality without compromising training time or model size [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Enhances geometric precision, stabilizes densification, reduces model size, faster training.
+*   **Limitations:** Requires SfM points for initial scale calculation. May struggle with very large, complex backgrounds if not tuned.
+*   **When to Use:** When high-fidelity geometric reconstruction is critical, especially for scenes with intricate details.
+*   **When Not to Use:** When SfM points are unavailable or poor quality.
 
 ## Background
-Atomized Proliferation is part of the AtomGS method, proposed by Liu, Xu, Hu, Chen, and Feng. It was introduced to resolve issues in standard 3D Gaussian Splatting where Gaussians do not adhere to underlying geometric structures, leading to noisy 3D meshes and slower convergence [S1, unofficial]. The method combines atomized proliferation with geometry-guided optimization [S1, unofficial].
+Atomized Proliferation was introduced in the paper "AtomGS: Atomizing Gaussian Splatting for High-Fidelity Radiance Field" by Liu et al. [S1, unofficial]. It addresses issues in standard 3DGS where Gaussians may not adhere to underlying geometric structures, leading to noisy meshes, blurry artifacts, and slower convergence [S1, unofficial]. The method was developed to improve geometric precision by constraining Gaussians into Atom Gaussians and prioritizing their proliferation.
 
